Feature: A person flies a drone
  A drone always runs. A computer flies it: ArduPilot, in [[AGENT mode]], with the order and the
  [[queue]] that the [[commander]] gave. A person can fly it instead. The person is the drone's
  [[pilot]]. The pilot takes the seat of one drone, and the sticks of the pilot become the radio
  channels of that drone. The server still takes the drone off, and puts it on its wing. Then the
  pilot has it. Only a winged drone has a pilot. The light quad does not. A drone has one pilot.

  The pilot gets a [[mission]] on the screen. The mission is the order of the commander and its
  queue. The pilot decides how to fly it. The game does not check that the pilot obeys. The pilot
  also gets what the drone itself knows: what its camera sees, its battery and the time since it
  was last in contact. The pilot does not get the map of the team, and does not get the truth.

  The commander still sees a drone that a person flies. Its telemetry comes down like that of any
  other drone, so the map of the team shows where the drone is. The commander can go on giving
  it orders. The ground station of the team leaves the drone alone: it does not put it in AGENT
  mode and sends it no plan.

  In a radio shadow no order gets through to the drone. The mission on the pilot's screen does not
  change, as the order of a computer drone does not change. It changes when the radio reaches the
  drone again.

  If the sticks of the pilot stop for 2 seconds, or the pilot leaves the seat, the drone is the
  computer's again. The ground station puts it in AGENT mode, and the drone flies its order itself.

  The server's own tests check these rules with seat clients and no ArduPilot: the sticks reach the
  radio of the drone, the quad and a second pilot are refused, sticks that stop give the drone back,
  a seat that commands is told which drones are piloted, the mission follows an order in contact and
  stays in a radio shadow, and a pilot gets no map and no truth
  (`cargo test --release -p recreation_towers_server --bins` and `-p recreation_towers_ground`).
  They are not expectations here: a state that no driver of the suite can show would make the
  suite's coverage plans unsupported. The expectations below are the game on ArduPlane SITL with a
  scripted pilot.


  A scripted pilot takes the second Blue drone after 40 seconds of play, flies a gentle bank by the
  sticks for 25 seconds and lets go. The two computer commanders play on.

  Expectation: A drone that a person flies is not in AGENT mode, and it turns with the sticks
    The ground station of Blue leaves the drone alone while the pilot has it. Its telemetry still
    comes down, so it stays on the map of Blue.

    When two computer teams play a game of Towers while a person flies the second Blue drone
    Then the looks at the second Blue drone in AGENT mode while a person flies it are 0
    And the turn of the second Blue drone under its pilot is at least 45 degrees
    And the looks of the Blue ground at the second Blue drone on its map while a person flies it are at least 1

  Expectation: The pilot sees the order of the commander as a mission
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers while a person flies the second Blue drone
    Then the orders that the pilot of the second Blue drone saw as its mission are at least 1
    And the missions that changed on the pilot's screen out of contact are 0

  Expectation: When the pilot lets go, the drone flies its order in AGENT mode
    When two computer teams play a game of Towers while a person flies the second Blue drone
    Then the second Blue drone after the pilot let go is in AGENT mode with a plan
